lesson physics-light-to-see-by v=1 format=teach grade=1 unit=8 subject=physics levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Light to see by"
  goal "Say which things give out their own light, that we need light to see anything, and whether a sheet lets light through."

  look {
    scene 24x16 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 0) space=1 align=start {
        things t things=["candle", "torch", "lamp", "sun", "moon", "mirror"]
        seeing room lamp=1 arrows=1
      }
    }
    say """
      A candle, a torch, a lamp and the sun give out their own light. The moon and a mirror
      look bright, but they only send back light that came from somewhere else. To see the ball,
      light has to bounce off it and come into your eyes.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice physics.see-in-dark count=2 seed=1
    practice physics.sources count=2 seed=4
    practice physics.count-sources count=2 seed=3
    practice physics.light-through count=2 seed=2
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show physics.shiny-moon
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show physics.not-sources p="moon" q="mirror" r="torch" s="sun" easy-q="lamp" hard-r="foil"
  }
  remember {
    say "We need light to see. Dark is when there is no light."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Go into a room with the curtains shut and the light off, and wait a minute. Can you see the
    toy on the floor? Now open the curtain a crack. The moon is the hard one: it looks like a
    lamp in the sky, and it is lit by the sun, which the stretch questions come back to.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the thing that only sends light back is a mirror or a marble, and the tray of five holds one thing without light of its own, the moon."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the moon and foil are among the things, which shine but give no light of their own, and the tray of five holds three of them."
  }
}
